COMENTARIOS:
THE REPRESSION AGAINST WOMEN WHO REFUSED TO SUBMIT TO FORCED HETEROSEXUALITY.
Before the emergence of lesbian feminism (FL) in 1976 and lesbian-feminism (LF) in 1977, women who did not submit to forced heterosexuality were victims of:
- separation, confinement, cloistering, marginalization, segregation, apartheid and social ostracism; 
- humiliation, insults, offenses, tying up, hanging, stoning, beatings, mutilations and sexual rape by family members, relatives or strangers; 
- expulsion from the home, family, neighborhood, school, university, workplace, union, religion and others; 
- aggressive psychiatric rehabilitation, violent correctives, extreme hormonal medicalization, procedures with psychotropic, anxiolytic and antipsychotic drugs, electric shocks or lobotomies;
- religious excommunication and divine punishments; 
- arranged and forced marriages; 
- confinement in psychiatric hospitals or prisons; 
- gang rape and 
- murder by family members, relatives or strangers... 
for this reason, many of these women preferred suicide. 
In synthesis, a physical, social and psychic genocide (femicide).

DISASTERS OF THE ATTEMPT TO FORM A LESBIAN GROUP WITH HOMOSEXUAL WOMEN FROM THE BARS.
The first attempts to form a lesbian group with homosexual women contacted in bars were a total disaster:
- the first session could not be held because they arrived with bottles in hand and organized a big party until five in the morning with a loud scandal, fighting within the apartment and neighbor complaints;
- the second time they were assured they could consume alcohol after two hours of the session; they held out but immediately upon finishing their friends arrived, a big party was organized and it also ended at five with a big scandal and fighting but in the street;
- the third time they held out during the session, their friends arrived and the party lasted until six in the morning but with a more controlled scandal because the music volume had been lowered, and fighting but on the other corner of the street;
- the fourth time they held out during the session, their friends arrived and they were allowed a social gathering until one in the morning and, afterwards, they went to the bars;
- the fifth time those interested in working in a small feminist consciousness-raising group (PGAF) arrived an hour early and upon finishing the session the other friends arrived and they immediately ran to the bars.
In some sessions around thirty were present because no more fit in Cristina's apartment, and up to sixty as in Eli Vargaz's house and upon their friends arriving they had to leave the apartment because they did not fit, so they went to the bars. 
Most of these attendees were not interested in working in a feminist group; however, they and their friends would participate actively in the lesbian contingent called by Oikabeth (the second lesbian group) for the first lesbian and homosexual marches starting in 1978, a contingent that gathered around two hundred women, both homosexual and lesbian.

BEGINNING OF WORK WITH THE SMALL FEMINIST CONSCIOUSNESS-RAISING GROUP (PGAF) METHODOLOGY.
With some of the homosexual women interested in serious work, we began working with the methodology of the Small Feminist Consciousness-Raising Group (PGAF) or sexpol group (although very unstable due to ups and downs because of erratic or very dramatic personal situations). The method was theoretically as follows:
1) express and share personal painful and traumatic experiences due to the fact of not submitting to forced heterosexuality;
2) mirror said experiences in the group to discover that it was not an individual problem but a collective one, not personal but social;
3) dismantle myths and prejudices against those who did not submit to said heterosexuality expressed as: deviation, unnatural, biological alteration, psychosomatic pathology, mental illness, abnormality, criminality or sin, among others;
4) overcome the feeling that one was: a semi-man, a castrated man, a man trapped in a woman's body or a third sex and, therefore, overcome the irrepressible and frustrating desire for the penis or the desire to change sex;
5) discover how positive and wonderful it was to be a woman, reconciling with one's being, accepting one's body and psyche devalued by all patriarchal history;
6) deconstruction of the homosexual being (dehomosexualization) and reconstruction of the lesbian being (lesbianization);
7) overcoming sexual roles: masculine-feminine (butch-fem) as a reproduction of the system of domination and, therefore, the elimination of oppression and violent relationships among women that these roles implied;
8) political revaluation of being women from the reclamation of feminism, the discovery of its transformative potential of oppressive reality, revolutionary;
9) understand lesbianism as the most radical expression of the feminist movement in its struggle against patriarchy.
Obviously, this methodology could not be followed with rigor but it was attempted.
